          "title": "The Two-Minute Explanation",
          "content": "The final test of understanding is whether you can explain the book simply and fairly. Try this out loud or in writing.\n\n1. **One sentence of plot**: a young woman in 1920s Petrograd fights to live her own life while loving an aristocrat and being loved by a communist officer, and the system destroys all three.\n\n2. **One sentence of theme**: Rand says it is about the individual against the state, not just Soviet Russia.\n\n3. **One sentence of context**: written by an emigre who lived through it, published in 1936, revised in 1959, and filmed without permission in fascist Italy.\n\n4. **One sentence of criticism**: vivid and heartfelt, but its communists are mostly caricatures and its ethics are dramatized rather than argued.\n\n5. **One sentence of your own verdict**: what do you take, and what do you argue with?\n\nIf you can say all five, you have finished the course.",
